<Article title="8-Week Dead Hang Program: Consistency & Progress Reviews" description="A free 8-week dead hang planning framework with review points, editable timer settings and a local training log. No guaranteed results or paid signup." schema={`<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"8-Week Dead Hang Program: Consistency & Progress Reviews","description":"A free 8-week dead hang planning framework with review points, editable timer settings and a local training log. No guaranteed results or paid signup.","dateModified":"2026-10-30","mainEntityOfPage":"https://deadhangs.com/8-week-program/","author":{"@id":"https://deadhangs.com/#organization"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://deadhangs.com/#organization"}}</script>`}>
<div class="article-header"><div class="article-breadcrumb"><a href="/training-programs/">Training plans</a><span class="sep">/</span>8 weeks</div><h1 class="article-h1">8-week dead hang plan.</h1><p class="article-subtitle">Organize your sessions around a repeatable baseline. This planning framework suits you once you can hold a comfortable 20 to 30 seconds. It is not a promise to reach a particular hold time.</p><p class="small-copy">Updated October 30, 2026</p></div>
<div class="article-body"><h2>Before the first session</h2><p>Use a securely fitted bar and a stable way to step down. Choose an assisted or unassisted hold you can control without pain. Keep that variation consistent when comparing results. If you have an injury or concerns about the movement, seek individual guidance.</p><h2>Your 8-week framework</h2>{blocks.map(([week,title,body])=><section class="plan-option"><p class="dh-eyebrow">{week}</p><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></section>)}<h2 id="session-structure">Keep a stable weekly slot</h2><p>Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days, for example Monday, Wednesday and Friday. Add a session only if you stay pain free. Use the first two weeks to see whether those slots fit your wider activity. Keep a familiar hold duration and support level before trying to add work.</p><p><strong>Worked example week:</strong> a starting point you adjust from your own baseline. Do 3 sets per session at a hold you can repeat cleanly. Rest 60 to 90 seconds between sets. Step down a second or two before your grip gives out. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p><ol><li>Repeat your setup and do a short warm-up of arm circles, wrist circles and one or two easy hangs.</li><li>Run your chosen hang, rest and set intervals. Record early finishes honestly.</li><li>Review two comparable sessions before changing the setup. Choose one adjustment: duration, assistance, sets or rest.</li><li>Keep the other variables stable through the next review. Return to the earlier settings if control worsens.</li></ol><p><strong>Example review:</strong> two sessions use the same bar and three 15-second supported holds. In session three you extend the hold to 18 seconds and keep assistance and rest unchanged. The example demonstrates a comparison method; it does not establish an optimal increase or guarantee progress.</p><h2>Choose your timer settings</h2><p>Enter your own hang duration, rest duration and number of sets. The timer on the training log page shows example defaults you can change. You can shorten a hold or increase rest before starting. The timer pauses when the page is hidden and never saves a session automatically.</p><a class="dh-btn" href="/training-log/">Open the timer & log</a><h2>Record the work you actually did</h2><p>Save the date, assistance, best actual hold and number of sets. Compare supported hangs with supported hangs and unassisted hangs with unassisted hangs. Export the log before clearing your browser or switching devices.</p><h2>When the plan needs changing</h2><p>Stop a painful hold. End a set before you lose the ability to step down under control. A missed session does not require making up extra sets. Repeating a block is an option; longer holds are not the only reason to keep a training record.</p><h2>Review the block</h2><p>Compare your current hold with an earlier hold made under the same conditions. The comparison with your own earlier hold is the number that matters for this plan.</p><p>The <a href="/dead-hang-calculator/">personal calculator</a> shows the difference without inventing a population percentile. The printable session planner on the training plans page suits you if you prefer paper. Dead hangs are one part of a wider routine; the <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/exercise/physical-activity-guidelines-for-adults-aged-19-to-64/">NHS activity guidance</a> covers broader strength and aerobic activity.</p></div>
